  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nClear skies have taken over across CWA after a little fog was seen \nearlier this morning near TRI and across our southern counties. High \npressure continues to dominate our forecast for the foreseeable \nfuture. This spells temperatures a degree or two above normal during \nthe afternoons and cooler mornings with patchy fog. The biggest \nchallenge over the next several days will be dew points and dry air \naloft mixing down to the surface pushing RH values lower than what \nNBM guidance would otherwise suggest. As far as fire weather goes, \nthe saving grace of light winds throughout the period will mitigate \nany great concerns. As dry as it is and has been, we'd be having a \nwidespread fire danger if it wasn't for these light winds.\n\nWhere's all the precipitation? Well, it currently resides out west, \nespecially over the Four Corners region and is being blocked from \ngetting to us by this high pressure over the center of the country. \nLong range guidance would suggest that this blocking pattern will \nstart to break down by the middle of next week with our first chance \nof rain potentially getting to us by next weekend -- just in time \nfor Halloween. While that sounds ghoulish, that solution is only in \nthe GFS. The Euro keeps us dry right through Halloween weekend. For \nnow, enjoy this consistently comfortable weather. It's bound to end \neventually.",
